\pard\qc\b\fs36 Ubitron Mobility Axiom\par
\pard\qc\b\fs28 Technical Manual\par
\pard\qc\fs20 On-Premises configuration and operations\par
\pard\qc\fs20 Audience: Customer IT administrators\par
\pard\qc\fs20 Language: English\par
\pard\qc\fs20 Version 2.1\par
\pard\qc\fs20 Revision date: July 2026\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b Part A \emdash Orientation\par
\pard\sa240\par

\pard\b 1. About this manual\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b 1.1 Purpose\par
\pard This Technical Manual is the IT reference for Ubitron Mobility Axiom after the server is installed and licensed. It explains how to configure Server Config, register body-worn cameras, plan firewall rules, manage evidence storage, operate the service, and recover from common faults.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 1.2 Who should use this manual\par
\pard 1. Customer IT administrators who own the production server\par
\pard 2. System integrators configuring networking, cameras, and storage\par
\pard 3. Site technical leads who create operator accounts and maintain the runbook\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard Control-room operators should use the Quick Start Guide and User Manual for daily work. They do not need this book for routine dispatch tasks.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 1.3 How to use this manual\par
\pard 1. Complete the Installation Guide first on a new site.\par
\pard 2. Use Part B in order for a first full configuration.\par
\pard 3. Use later chapters as reference when you change SIP, firewall, storage, or users.\par
\pard 4. Every major procedure includes Purpose, Before you start, Steps, Expected result, Notes, and Common problems.\par
\pard 5. Screenshot placeholders mark where product screens will appear in the published PDF.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 1.4 Related manuals\par
\pard 1. Installation Guide (On-Premises) \emdash service install, Setup, license file, first administrator password, handoff\par
\pard 2. Quick Start Guide \emdash first operator tasks after handoff\par
\pard 3. User Manual \emdash daily control-room use\par
\pard 4. Managed Cloud and Hybrid installation guides \emdash when your contract is not pure on-premises\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 1.5 Out of scope\par
\pard 1. Day-to-day operator procedures (User Manual)\par
\pard 2. First-time Windows Server or Linux service install steps (Installation Guide)\par
\pard 3. Ubitron internal license signing tools\par
\pard 4. Migration from an older site (Migration Guide)\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 2. System architecture\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b 2.1 Components\par
\pard Mobility Axiom is a server application that runs as a background service on Windows Server or a supported Linux host.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard The production site typically includes:\par
\pard 1. The Mobility Axiom server service (always on)\par
\pard 2. Operator workstations that open a web browser to the Operator portal URL\par
\pard 3. Body-worn cameras that register to the server Device registration IPv4 and SIP port\par
\pard 4. Evidence storage on local disk or a NAS/SAN mount prepared by IT\par
\pard 5. Optional reverse proxy and TLS termination in front of the portal (WAN or hardened LAN designs)\par
\pard 6. Optional licensed modules (for example PTT, video conference, analytics, \b CAD / RMS\b0  \emdash Computer-Aided Dispatch / Records Management) when your license includes them\par
\pard 7. Supporting services on some sites (container engine and related helpers) \emdash see Chapter 18\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 2.2 How traffic flows (plain language)\par
\pard 1. Operators reach the dashboard through the Operator portal URL.\par
\pard 2. Cameras reach the server through Device registration IPv4 and the SIP port shown in Settings.\par
\pard 3. Live video and related media use additional ports listed in the firewall readiness checklist.\par
\pard 4. Evidence files land in the storage paths you configure under Evidence.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard These are three different doors. Mixing them is the most common network mistake on new sites. Chapter 15 explains each door in detail.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 2.3 What operators never do\par
\pard Operators do not start Mobility Axiom from a desktop window each day. They do not open Setup. They do not manage supporting container services. They sign in through the browser only.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert diagram: Server service, operator browsers, body-worn cameras, evidence storage]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 3. Roles and access\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b 3.1 Super administrator\par
\pard The super administrator can open Settings \emdash Server Config, change networking and SIP values, manage users, and change evidence storage. Protect this account in your password vault.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard On a new install, the first administrator account is typically global. After the mandatory password change, continue to use global as the site super administrator unless your policy creates a different named account with the same rights.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 3.2 Operators\par
\pard Operators use the portal for live video, maps, evidence review, and other licensed features. They normally cannot change Server Config. When a non\emdash super-admin opens Server Config, fields appear read-only.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 3.3 Recommended separation\par
\pard 1. Keep super administrator credentials for IT only.\par
\pard 2. Create named operator accounts for dispatch staff.\par
\pard 3. Do not share the global password with the whole control room.\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 4. Setup Mode versus production dashboard\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Understand when the server is in Setup and when it is in normal production so you do not send operators to the wrong URL.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 4.1 Setup Mode\par
\pard Setup appears when a valid license is not yet installed, or when the server must stop for a start-up fault that only Setup can clear.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard Setup is available on the server itself at:\par
\pard http://127.0.0.1:13988\par
\pard (Use the Setup port from the service log if it differs.)\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard You need the 6-digit Setup PIN from the service log (search for ACTION REQUIRED or Setup Mode). The PIN changes each time Setup starts.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Important\par
\pard Do not publish Setup to the public internet. Remote Desktop or a secure tunnel to localhost on the server is acceptable.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard Full first-time Setup and license install steps are in the Installation Guide.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 4.2 Production dashboard\par
\pard After a valid license and network mode are in place, operators and administrators use the dashboard on the package dashboard ports (often HTTP 3988 unless your README states otherwise), or the Operator portal URL you configure in Settings.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Daily staff bookmark the Operator portal URL. IT uses Setup only for license and first-start recovery tasks.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Setup page on localhost]\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Dashboard login page]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 5. Licensing for IT (day-2)\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Keep the site license healthy after go-live and know what to do when Hardware ID or entitlements change.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 5.1 How licensing works\par
\pard 1. The license file is locked to this server Hardware ID.\par
\pard 2. Ubitron issues the signed license file. You do not create or sign licenses on the customer server.\par
\pard 3. Install the license on the Setup page (not on the daily Operations dashboard).\par
\pard 4. Do not edit the license file. Editing breaks the signature.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 5.2 Entitlements\par
\pard Your license may enable optional modules such as PTT, redaction, analytics, video conference, tactical views, or \b CAD / RMS\b0  (Computer-Aided Dispatch / Records Management) \emdash an optional integration. Features that are not licensed appear locked in the product. Contact Ubitron to change entitlements; do not try to unlock features by editing files.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 5.3 If the license is rejected\par
\pard 1. Confirm the Hardware ID on Setup still matches the ID you sent to Ubitron.\par
\pard 2. Confirm the file was not edited or re-saved in a way that changes contents.\par
\pard 3. Contact Ubitron with the Hardware ID and the exact error text.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard First-time license request and install steps remain in the Installation Guide, Chapter 5.\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b Part B \emdash Server Config\par
\pard\sa240\par

\pard\b 6. Server Config overview\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Server Config is the control surface for site identity, networking, portal access, camera protocol, resiliency, and readiness.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 6.1 How to open Server Config\par
\pard 1. Sign in as super administrator.\par
\pard 2. Open Settings.\par
\pard 3. Open Server Config.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 6.2 Main areas\par
\pard Under Network and deployment you work through six phases:\par
\pard 1. Identity\par
\pard 2. Networking\par
\pard 3. Access and Security\par
\pard 4. Storage and Devices\par
\pard 5. Resiliency\par
\pard 6. Diagnostics\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard Related Server Config areas (sibling tabs):\par
\pard 1. BWCs \emdash device list for body-worn cameras\par
\pard 2. Map groups \emdash grouping for map operations\par
\pard 3. Dashboard Authentication \emdash users, roles, and site security\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 6.3 Save and restart rule\par
\pard After you save network or SIP changes in Server Config, restart the Mobility Axiom service so cameras and operators pick up the new details. Then update firewall rules and update each camera that already uses the old values.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Important\par
\pard Saving alone is not enough for SIP or device IP changes. Restart, firewall, and Type on BWC must follow.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Server Config with six phase navigation]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 7. Phase 1 \emdash Identity\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Record how this site is deployed and optionally name the tenant or customer for this server.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Before you start\par
\pard 1. Know whether this server is on-premises LAN, WAN public edge, or a Ubitron managed/hybrid design.\par
\pard 2. Have super administrator access.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Steps\par
\pard 1. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash Identity.\par
\pard 2. Set Deployment type to match this site.\par
\pard 3. For a typical on-premises control room on the private network, use the LAN server style deployment described on screen.\par
\pard 4. If your contract is Ubitron Managed Cloud or Hybrid, stop and use that installation guide instead of forcing cloud settings into an on-premises-only runbook.\par
\pard 5. Optionally enter Tenant / customer name.\par
\pard 6. Save.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Identity fields match the real site model. Later phases show hints that match that model.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Notes\par
\pard Deployment type changes how the product describes Device registration IPv4 and Operator portal URL. It does not replace correct firewall design.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Common problems\par
\pard 1. Choosing a cloud-style deployment for a pure LAN site confuses portal and device addressing. Match the real design.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Identity phase]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 8. Phase 2 \emdash Networking\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Set LAN and WAN planning fields and, most importantly, the Device registration IPv4 that body-worn cameras must dial.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Before you start\par
\pard 1. Record the server real LAN IPv4 from the operating system (Ethernet preferred).\par
\pard 2. Confirm you are not copying a virtual adapter address in the range 172.17 to 172.31.\par
\pard 3. Know subnet, gateway, and DNS if you document static addressing.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Steps\par
\pard 1. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash Networking.\par
\pard 2. Under LAN Network, set IP assignment (Static IPv4 or DHCP) to match how the host OS is configured.\par
\pard 3. Enter Server IP Address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS to match the OS when you use static addressing. If DHCP is used, record the assigned address accurately.\par
\pard 4. Optionally enter Server hostname.\par
\pard 5. Under WAN / Internet, complete public IPv4, DDNS, edge router, or VPN endpoint only when your site design uses them.\par
\pard 6. Set Device registration IPv4 to the address cameras must type. On a LAN site this is normally the server real LAN IPv4.\par
\pard 7. Review SIP listen address. Use 0.0.0.0 to listen on all interfaces unless your multi-NIC design requires a specific LAN IP.\par
\pard 8. Save.\par
\pard 9. Restart the Mobility Axiom service.\par
\pard 10. Update firewall rules if the device address or related ports changed.\par
\pard 11. Update Type on BWC values on every camera that was already programmed.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Device registration IPv4 matches the address cameras can reach on the real network. Operator bookmarks are not required to equal this field.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Important\par
\pard 1. Device registration IPv4 must be IPv4 only (digits and dots). Body-worn keypads cannot enter hostnames.\par
\pard 2. Never use 172.17 to 172.31 for Device registration IPv4 or operator bookmarks. Those ranges are commonly used by virtualisation and container adapters and will break field devices.\par
\pard 3. Operator portal URL is configured in Access and Security, not as a substitute for Device registration IPv4.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Common problems\par
\pard 1. Cameras offline because Device registration IPv4 points at a laptop Wi-Fi guest address that changed.\par
\pard 2. Cameras offline because IT pasted a Docker or WSL virtual address.\par
\pard 3. Portal works from IT PC but cameras cannot route to the server IP \emdash routing or VLAN issue, not a failed save.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Networking phase with Device registration IPv4]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 9. Phase 3 \emdash Access and Security\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Set the Operator portal URL staff will bookmark, and configure reverse-proxy trust and certificates when your design terminates HTTPS in front of Mobility Axiom.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Before you start\par
\pard 1. Decide the exact URL dispatch will use (for example https://dispatch.example.com).\par
\pard 2. Know whether TLS stops at nginx, Caddy, IIS, or another reverse proxy in front of this server.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Steps\par
\pard 1. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash Access and Security.\par
\pard 2. Set Operator portal URL to the address staff will bookmark.\par
\pard 3. If HTTPS terminates on a reverse proxy in front of this server, turn Trust reverse proxy ON, then Save.\par
\pard 4. If operators connect directly to the application TLS listener with no front proxy, leave Trust reverse proxy OFF unless your package instructions say otherwise.\par
\pard 5. When your package supports on-prem certificate upload, use the Certificate and Private key controls as described on screen and in the package README.\par
\pard 6. Note the Dashboard port value shown as runtime information. This listen port is usually fixed at install time.\par
\pard 7. Save.\par
\pard 8. Restart the service if network access behaviour changed.\par
\pard 9. From an operator workstation, open the Operator portal URL and confirm the login page loads.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Staff have one clear bookmark. Trust reverse proxy matches the real TLS design. Dashboard port display matches the running service.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Notes \emdash portal URL versus listen port\par
\pard 1. Operator portal URL is the human door. It may hide the raw port behind HTTPS on 443 through a reverse proxy.\par
\pard 2. Dashboard listen port is what the Mobility Axiom service binds on the host. It often appears read-only in Settings.\par
\pard 3. To change the listen port, follow the delivery package README (environment or package setting), then restart the service. Do not expect a daily toggle inside Server Config for that value.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Common problems\par
\pard 1. Bookmark uses http while only https is published \emdash browser fails.\par
\pard 2. Trust reverse proxy OFF while nginx terminates TLS \emdash login or redirect behaviour looks wrong.\par
\pard 3. Staff still use the raw server IP and port after you published a clean hostname \emdash update the runbook and retrain bookmarks.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Operator portal URL and Trust reverse proxy]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 10. Phase 4 \emdash Storage and Devices\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Configure camera protocol values (especially SIP), use Type on BWC as the device programming checklist, set site timezone, and open the path to Evidence storage configuration.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Before you start\par
\pard 1. Device registration IPv4 is already correct in Networking.\par
\pard 2. You know whether cameras must use a non-default SIP port for this network.\par
\pard 3. Super administrator access is available.\par
\pard\sa120\par

\pard\b 10.1 Protocol and Type on BWC\par
\pard\b Steps\par
\pard 1. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash Storage and Devices.\par
\pard 2. Review SIP port, Platform / server ID, Realm, SIP register password, Alt SIP password, and Media transport.\par
\pard 3. Change SIP port only if your network design requires it.\par
\pard 4. Complete ONVIF fields only when your deployment uses ONVIF devices as described on screen.\par
\pard 5. Open Type on BWC. This checklist shows the server values each camera must receive on its SIP screen.\par
\pard 6. Set site timezone for this location.\par
\pard 7. Save.\par
\pard 8. If you changed SIP port, passwords, realm, media transport, or Device registration IPv4: restart the Mobility Axiom service.\par
\pard 9. Update host and upstream firewall rules to match the live firewall checklist.\par
\pard 10. On every body-worn camera, enter the Type on BWC values (server IP, SIP port, passwords, and related fields).\par
\pard 11. Confirm devices show Online when they are powered and in range.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Type on BWC matches what is programmed on devices. Cameras register. Live views can start for online units.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Important \emdash changing SIP port\par
\pard Use this full sequence every time:\par
\pard 1. Change SIP port in Storage and Devices.\par
\pard 2. Save.\par
\pard 3. Restart the Mobility Axiom service.\par
\pard 4. Update firewall rules for the new SIP port (and related media ports from the checklist).\par
\pard 5. Update every camera.\par
\pard 6. Verify Online status and one live view.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard Skipping camera updates after a SIP change is the most common cause of mass offline devices.\par
\pard\sa120\par

\pard\b 10.2 Evidence storage link\par
\pard Storage paths for dock FTP and live capture are configured under Evidence \emdash Storage. Use the link or navigation from this phase when shown, then follow Chapter 17.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Notes\par
\pard 1. Always type Device registration IPv4 and SIP port from live Settings, not from memory of another site.\par
\pard 2. Media transport (UDP or TCP) affects which media firewall rows you must open. Read the checklist after save.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Common problems\par
\pard 1. Password on camera does not match SIP register password.\par
\pard 2. Camera still points at old SIP port after a change.\par
\pard 3. Firewall still allows only the old SIP port.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Storage and Devices SIP fields]\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Type on BWC checklist]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 11. Phase 5 \emdash Resiliency\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Record node identity and peer information when your site design uses more than a single standalone server node.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Before you start\par
\pard Most on-premises sites run a single Mobility Axiom node. Complete this phase with defaults unless Ubitron or your design document specifies peer settings.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Steps\par
\pard 1. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash Resiliency.\par
\pard 2. Review Node ID and peer URL fields.\par
\pard 3. Enter peer values only when your approved design requires them.\par
\pard 4. Save site node settings.\par
\pard 5. Use local and peer health displays as status only.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Single-node sites show healthy local status. Peer fields remain empty or unused unless designed otherwise.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Notes\par
\pard Do not invent a multi-site topology in this screen without a Ubitron-approved design. Wrong peer URLs create confusing health states without improving cameras.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Resiliency phase]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 12. Phase 6 \emdash Diagnostics\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Use site configuration status and the readiness checklist to confirm the server is fit for go-live or fit after a change.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Before you start\par
\pard Complete Identity, Networking, Access and Security, and Storage and Devices for the values you intend to run.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Steps\par
\pard 1. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash Diagnostics.\par
\pard 2. Review Site configuration status.\par
\pard 3. Open the site readiness checklist.\par
\pard 4. Resolve each failing item (device IP, portal URL, proxy trust, and other checks named on screen).\par
\pard 5. Refresh after corrections.\par
\pard 6. When the checklist is acceptable for your go-live bar, continue to BWCs and acceptance testing.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard You can explain every remaining warning. Critical networking and portal checks pass before operator handoff.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Notes\par
\pard Diagnostics does not replace the firewall readiness checklist or a real camera Online test. Use all three.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Diagnostics readiness checklist]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b Part C \emdash Devices, users, storage, network doors\par
\pard\sa240\par

\pard\b 13. Body-worn cameras and map groups\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Register each camera in Mobility Axiom and keep device identity aligned with what is typed on the hardware.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Before you start\par
\pard 1. Device registration IPv4 and SIP values are saved.\par
\pard 2. Type on BWC checklist is current.\par
\pard 3. Firewall allows BWC SIP and related media ports.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 13.1 Register devices\par
\pard\b Steps\par
\pard 1. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash BWCs (or the BWCs area in Server Config).\par
\pard 2. Add each Device ID exactly as it will be entered on the camera.\par
\pard 3. Enter officer or unit name as required by site policy.\par
\pard 4. Assign map groups when your operations design uses them.\par
\pard 5. Save the BWC list.\par
\pard 6. On the physical camera, enter Type on BWC server values and the same Device ID.\par
\pard 7. Power the camera on the correct network segment.\par
\pard 8. Confirm the device shows Online in the product.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Registered devices can come Online. Operators can open live views for online units according to their role and license.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b 13.2 Map groups\par
\pard Map groups help organise units on the map and in operations views. Create groups that match real teams or districts. Incorrect groups rarely break registration, but they confuse dispatch.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Common problems\par
\pard 1. Device ID on camera does not match the BWC list.\par
\pard 2. SIP password mismatch.\par
\pard 3. Camera uses hostname; product requires IPv4 for registration.\par
\pard 4. Camera VLAN cannot route to Device registration IPv4.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: BWC list with Online device]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 14. Dashboard Authentication (IT)\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Create operator accounts, assign roles, and maintain site security settings after the first administrator sign-in.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b IMPORTANT \emdash First sign-in (new server)\par
\pard 1. On a new server, the first administrator account is typically username global with password global123.\par
\pard 2. Mobility Axiom requires a password change before normal use. You cannot keep the factory password.\par
\pard 3. After change, global123 stops working. Store the new password in the site vault.\par
\pard 4. Full first-sign-in steps are in the Installation Guide.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Steps \emdash create operators\par
\pard 1. Sign in as super administrator.\par
\pard 2. Open Settings \emdash Server Config \emdash Dashboard Authentication.\par
\pard 3. Create a user for each operator who needs access.\par
\pard 4. Assign the role that matches their duties.\par
\pard 5. Set or confirm site security options required by your policy.\par
\pard 6. If the product requires an IT PIN for Diagnostics or related elevated tools, set that PIN and store it separately from login passwords.\par
\pard 7. Save.\par
\pard 8. Have each operator sign in once and confirm their landing rights.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Expected result\par
\pard Operators work under named accounts. Super administrator credentials stay with IT.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Common problems\par
\pard 1. Operators share one password \emdash audit and lockout risk.\par
\pard 2. Operator account cannot open Server Config \emdash expected for non\emdash super-admin.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Dashboard Authentication user list]\par
\pard\sa360\par

\pard\b 15. Ports and network doors\par
\pard\b0\par
\pard\b Purpose\par
\pard Give IT a single clear model of how portal access, camera registration, and service listen ports relate.\par
\pard\sa120\par

\pard\b 15.1 Three doors\par
\pard\b Door A \emdash Operator portal URL\par
\pard What staff type in the browser. Set in Access and Security. May be an HTTPS hostname on 443 through a reverse proxy.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Door B \emdash Device registration\par
\pard Device registration IPv4 plus SIP port (and related Type on BWC fields). Cameras use this door. Set in Networking and Storage and Devices.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Door C \emdash Dashboard listen ports\par
\pard The ports the Mobility Axiom service binds on the host. Often shown read-only as Dashboard port in Settings. Changed through package or environment settings per README, then service restart.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard\b Warning\par
\pard Do not assume Door A, Door B, and Door C are the same string. On many professional sites they intentionally differ.\par
\pard\sa120\par

\pard\b 15.2 Typical defaults (confirm in README and live Settings)\par
\pard 1. Setup (server only): 13988 \emdash localhost; not for operators\par
\pard 2. Dashboard HTTP: often 3988 \emdash package may differ\par
\pard 3. Dashboard HTTPS: package-specific\par
\pard 4. SIP (cameras): the value shown in Settings / Type on BWC\par
\pard 5. Live video WebSocket, live audio WebSocket, Message Center, RTP ranges, PTT, FTP, and related rows: read from the live firewall checklist after your settings are saved\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard Always prefer live Settings and the firewall checklist over memory of another site.\par
\pard\sa120\par

\pard\b 15.3 After any SIP or network save\par
\pard 1. Restart the Mobility Axiom service.\par
\pard 2. Update firewall rules from the live checklist.\par
\pard 3. Update Type on BWC on every affected camera.\par
\pard 4. Verify Online status and one live view.\par
\pard\sa120\par
\pard [Insert screenshot: Firewall readiness checklist with BWC SIP row]\par
